 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S441-014\", \"clauses\": {\"determine\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F06\", \"template\": \"Look at the calendar page shown. What month of the year is shown on this calendar?\", \"reasoning\": \"F06 has no slots. It asks the learner to determine the month of the year from a calendar page shown, which is the determine task applied to the month of the year.\"}, \"day_and_month_of_the_year\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F14\", \"template\": \"Look at the {month_name} {year} {cal_word}. What day of the week is {month_name} {date}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F14 names a month from the fixed month list and asks a day question about that month, so it addresses the day and month of the year. F14 is also a failing family because its date slot is not tied to the month, so it can print impossible dates.\"}, \"calendar\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F13\", \"template\": \"Look at the {cal_word} page shown. What month of the year is shown on this {cal_word}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F13 places the calendar representation in the question. cal_word draws only from F01 and F15, which both print the word calendar.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F05\", \"F07\", \"F08\", \"F09\", \"F14\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"Count to the {month}th month.\", \"Look at the {v} {year} calendar. What day of the week is {v} {target_date}?\", \"The {elapsed_label} is the number of days or weeks that have passed.\", \"The {months_label} go from January (1st) to December (12th).\", \"Look at the {month_name} {year} {cal_word}. What day of the week is {month_name} {date}?\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 template Count to the {month}th month. has month printed as every number from 1 to 12 (printed_runs 1 to 12), so the month values 1, 2 and 3 produce the malformed ordinals 1th, 2th and 3th. F07 and F14 pair a month name with a date from 1 to 31 with no link between them (target_date printed_runs 1 to 31, derived only from target_date), so February can print 30 or 31 and other short months can print 31, which are impossible dates in 2025. F08 template The {elapsed_label} is the number of days or weeks that have passed. takes elapsed_label only from nested F02, which always prints how much time has passed, so the hint reads as an ungrammatical sentence, and it introduces the days-or-weeks elapsed concept. F09 template The {months_label} go from January (1st) to December (12th). takes months_label from F03, which prints the 12 months, so the hint reads The the 12 months, a duplicated article. It also prints the ordinal 12th, while cumulative_vocab gives ordinals only up to 10th. F06, F13, F01, F10, F11, F12 and F15 raised no defect in the packet.\"}",
